  "query_preview": "..\n\nPersistent lake effect cloud cover this evening gradually erodes \nthrough the night as Midwestern surface high pressure fully builds \nover lower MI. With clearing occuring west/northwest to east, \ncoolest areas with lows falling into upper 20s/near 30 favored \ntowards the Tri-Cities and west of I-75/US-23. Eastern area's later \nclearing offers a slightly warmer night with lows in the low to mid \n30s.\n\nWork week is dominated by central CONUS ridging slowly sliding \nacross the Great Lakes through early Thursday providing another \nprolonged mild, dry stretch of weather. Surface high pressure \nsettles over the eastern Ohio Valley by Monday allowing \nsouthwesterly low level winds to develop for SE MI. These winds \ncontinue through midweek offering long duration weak warm advection \ninto southern lower MI slowly but steadily warming temps. Highs \nMonday top out a few degrees above normal in the lower 50s however \nmid 50s return by Tuesday with 60s possible by Thursday- some 10-15 \ndegrees above normal. Lighter winds early week turn breezier midweek \nas the gradient tightens in response to low pressure lifting across \nthe northern Plains offering potential >20mph gusts by Wednesday and \n>30mph gusts Thursday. Aforementioned low favored to track over the \nfar northern Great Lakes Thursday which, as it currently stands, is \ntoo far north to see any rain locally. Should this scenario occur, \nnext chances for SE MI to see any rainfall won't arrive until next \nweekend.",
